Cell["\<\
The phase behaviour of biomolecular condensates, generated live here: (left) \
the interaction\[Dash]chain-length plane that decides whether a mixture \
demixes at all, and (right) how strongly a condensate accelerates the \
reactions inside it. Beneath it all runs a single idea \[LongDash] the same \
Flory\[Dash]Huggins physics that unmixes oil from water also organises the \
living cell, and may have lit the first spark of life.\
\>", "Text",
 FontSize->12,
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.4],ExpressionUUID->"731b8765-c68d-442a-bed2-8a230b10ae78"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["1.  The membrane-free organelle", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"eb89e456-e453-4fea-a3ba-739853a58562"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For over a century, cell biology was built on the assumption that the \
cell's internal organisation depends on ",
 StyleBox["membranes",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ": lipid bilayers that enclose the nucleus, the mitochondria, the \
endoplasmic reticulum. Compartmentalisation by membranes was the organising \
principle of eukaryotic life."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"154ac9d5-3683-4f11-b875-040687c1ae1e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Then, in 2009, ",
 StyleBox["Brangwynne, Eckmann, Courson, Rybarska, Hoege, Gharakhani, J\
\[UDoubleDot]licher, and Hyman",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " showed that P granules \[LongDash] RNA\[Dash]protein bodies in the germ \
cells of the nematode ",
 StyleBox["C. elegans",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " \[LongDash] are not membrane-bound organelles at all. They are ",
 StyleBox["liquid droplets",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ". They flow, they fuse, they drip, they dissolve and re-form. They are \
phase-separated condensates of RNA and proteins, held together not by a lipid \
membrane but by the same physics that separates oil from water."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2f564ede-690b-42c6-8e78-a2ced928fd13"],

Cell["\<\
This discovery launched a revolution. Within a decade, dozens of cellular \
structures were reclassified as condensates: stress granules, nucleoli, Cajal \
bodies, PML bodies, centrosomes, synaptic densities, heterochromatin domains, \
transcriptional hubs. Protein science has been transformed in parallel \
\[LongDash] the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry went to David Baker for \
computational protein design and jointly to Demis Hassabis and John Jumper \
for protein-structure prediction \[LongDash] and the condensate revolution \
adds a complementary lesson: that proteins are not only rigid machines but \
can phase-separate into functional liquids.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c160204b-afe1-454b-8cba-5f338155410a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["The physics.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " The formation of a condensate is a thermodynamic phase transition. Below a \
critical temperature (or above a critical interaction strength), a \
homogeneous solution of macromolecules becomes thermodynamically unstable and \
spontaneously separates into a dilute phase and a dense phase. The dense \
phase is the condensate. The transition is governed by a competition between \
the entropy of mixing (which favours homogeneity) and the enthalpic \
interactions between molecules (which favour like-with-like aggregation). The \
mathematical framework for this competition is ",
 StyleBox["Flory\[Dash]Huggins theory",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ", which we derive in the next section."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"945ee149-e80a-4684-8845-7f6c6f457522"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["The origin-of-life connection.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Perhaps the most provocative implication of the condensate revolution is \
its connection to the ",
 StyleBox["origin of life",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ". Aleksandr Oparin proposed (in 1924, and in molecular detail in his 1936 \
book) that life began inside ",
 StyleBox["coacervate droplets",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " \[LongDash] spontaneously forming colloidal structures that concentrate \
organic molecules from the dilute primordial ocean. Oparin's hypothesis was \
dismissed for decades because the known coacervates were too disordered to \
sustain biochemistry. But modern condensates, with their exquisite \
sequence-dependent phase behaviour and their ability to concentrate specific \
clients by factors of up to a thousand, revive Oparin's vision in molecular \
detail. We show in \[Section]4 that condensate-assisted chemistry solves the ",
 StyleBox["concentration problem",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " that has bedevilled prebiotic chemistry: how do you get reactions going in \
a picomolar ocean?"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ce84fcc2-7447-4f2e-9d57-58417f9ecc8d"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["2.  Thermodynamics of phase separation", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"22b84df6-c453-4765-8e3a-41307efaf37a"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["2.1  The Flory\[Dash]Huggins free energy", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"d38dd0da-3f6a-45af-9f10-1de971c53771"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["The lattice model.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Consider a lattice of ",
 StyleBox["N\:209c",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " sites, each occupied by either a monomer of species 1 (the polymer, chain \
length ",
 StyleBox["N\:2081",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ") or a monomer of species 2 (the solvent, with ",
 StyleBox["N\:2082 = 1",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 "). Let ",
 StyleBox["\[Phi]",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " be the volume fraction of species 1. The Flory\[Dash]Huggins free energy \
of mixing per lattice site is:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"cab18e97-4863-4c25-a8a2-17f16813364a"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   FractionBox["f", "kT"], "=", 
   FractionBox[
    RowBox[{"\[Phi]", " ", 
     RowBox[{"ln", " ", "\[Phi]"}]}], 
    SubscriptBox["N", "1"]], "+", 
   FractionBox[
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"1", "-", "\[Phi]"}], ")", " ", 
     RowBox[{"ln", "(", 
      RowBox[{"1", "-", "\[Phi]"}], ")"}]}], 
    SubscriptBox["N", "2"]], "+", 
   RowBox[{"\[Chi]", " ", "\[Phi]", "(", 
    RowBox[{"1", "-", "\[Phi]"}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "DisplayFormula",\

 CellMargins->{{80, 50}, {8, 
  8}},ExpressionUUID->"ccae85b2-5369-47b0-aebf-19141f46038f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The first two terms are the ",
 StyleBox["entropy of mixing",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " \[LongDash] always negative (favour mixing) and scaled by the inverse \
chain lengths ",
 StyleBox["1/N\:2081",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["1/N\:2082",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ". Longer chains have less translational entropy per monomer, so polymers \
phase-separate more readily than small molecules. The third term is the ",
 StyleBox["enthalpy of mixing",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ", parameterised by the Flory interaction parameter ",
 StyleBox["\[Chi]",
  FontFamily->"Times",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ". For many condensate-forming proteins, \[Chi] > 0 (unfavourable mixing) \
and \[Chi] decreases with temperature (\[Chi] \[Proportional] 1/T), giving an \
upper critical solution temperature (UCST) phase diagram \[LongDash] though a \
substantial class of intrinsically disordered proteins (e.g. elastin-like \
sequences) instead show the opposite, lower critical solution temperature \
(LCST), behaviour."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"fb0e90d3-0791-481a-a06e-f71d146098d9"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
